WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.481
Require authenticated access at the cluster boundary and authorize clients for specific models or service classes.

00:00:06.481 --> 00:00:10.868
Protect APIs with encryption in transit, rate limits, and network controls.

00:00:10.868 --> 00:00:13.261
Segment user-facing gateways, management interfaces, storage,

00:00:13.261 --> 00:00:19.243
and worker traffic so compromise of one component does not expose the entire control plane.

00:00:19.243 --> 00:00:23.231
Store service credentials in a managed secrets system, rotate them,

00:00:23.231 --> 00:00:26.421
and avoid embedding them in images or scripts.

00:00:26.421 --> 00:00:29.612
Patch operating systems, GPU drivers, runtimes, front ends,

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:32.403
and orchestration components through a controlled process.

00:00:32.403 --> 00:00:37.588
Remove one node from service, apply the change, test health and representative requests,

00:00:37.588 --> 00:00:41.177
and expand the rollout only when results are acceptable.

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:47.558
Maintain rollback packages and configuration snapshots because a syntactically successful update can still change model behavior,

00:00:47.558 --> 00:00:49.153
memory use, or throughput.

00:00:49.153 --> 00:00:52.344
Monitor from the request down to the hardware.

00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:57.129
Track availability, error rates, queue time, time to first token, generation rate,

00:00:57.129 --> 00:00:58.326
and end-to-end latency.

00:00:58.326 --> 00:01:03.111
Correlate those signals with GPU utilization, VRAM, CPU, RAM, disk, network, power,

00:01:03.111 --> 00:01:04.308
temperature, and throttling.

00:01:04.308 --> 00:01:09.093
Health checks should detect whether a process is merely running and whether

00:01:09.093 --> 00:01:11.885
it can actually answer a model request.

00:01:11.885 --> 00:01:17.070
Alert on user impact and sustained resource conditions rather than every brief fluctuation.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.266
Prepare for incidents.

00:01:18.266 --> 00:01:22.653
Preserve useful logs without retaining sensitive prompts longer than policy allows.

00:01:22.653 --> 00:01:27.040
Define how to isolate a node, revoke credentials, stop abusive traffic,

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.230
restore a model version, and communicate service degradation.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:33.022
Review cost and energy consumption alongside performance;

00:01:33.022 --> 00:01:36.212
idle accelerators and overloaded cooling are operational signals.

00:01:36.212 --> 00:01:41.796
A secure cluster is one whose normal state is visible, whose changes are controlled,

00:01:41.796 --> 00:01:46.980
and whose operators can recover from failure without improvising access or data handling.
